(of the Connecticut Valley halting their ploughs in the furrow, and) Tj T*
(straightening up to stare at a certain extraordinary vehicle going north) Tj T*
(on the river road. This vehicle was nothing less than a two-wheeled) Tj T*
(sulky, then a rig almost unheard of outside the towns, and one never) Tj T*
(known to be used by travellers. A sulky with bundle baggage lashed) Tj T*
(behind, surely the driver must be an odd kind of rogue! Stopping at) Tj T*
(nightfall at a farm, the stranger met with close scrutiny by rural) Tj T*
(candle light. He was a fair-haired youth an inch or so under six feet) Tj T*
(tall, and of that ?rangy? and powerful build which is as characteristic) Tj T*
(of American soil as Indian corn. His eyes, which were well spaced in a) Tj T*
(wide forehead, were grey-blue in color, he had a good chin to face the) Tj T*
(world with, and something of a lean and eagle-ish nose. His name, he) Tj T*
(said, was John Ledyard, and he was on his way to become a missionary to) Tj T*
(the Indians.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(This youth, John Ledyard, third of his name, had seen the light of day in) Tj T*
(the village of Groton, Connecticut; his father, a sea captain, had died) Tj T*
(young; legal mischance or a descent of harpy relatives had deprived the) Tj T*
(young mother of her property, and John had been brought up in the house) Tj T*
(of his grandfather at Hartford. Then had come years at grammar school,) Tj T*
(the death of his grandfather, his virtual adoption by an uncle and) Tj T*
(aunt, and the attempt of these good folk to make a lawyer of him, which) Tj T*
(experiment had not been a success.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(At twenty-one years of age, John presented something of a problem to his) Tj T*
(kinsmen. What was to be done with this great fair-haired youth who had) Tj T*
(neither money nor influential friends? Suddenly Destiny came down the) Tj T*
(Connecticut Valley with a letter.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: JOHN LEDYARD) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Courtesy Judge John A. Aiken._]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The Reverend Eleazar Wheelock, founder of Dartmouth, wrote to John) Tj T*
(inviting him to the college. The passion of this good man?s life was the) Tj T*
(evangelization of the dispossessed and incorrigible redskins; he visited) Tj T*
(them in their forlorn and dwindling encampments; he took their young) Tj T*
(men to be his pupils, and he had founded his college largely for the) Tj T*
(sake of training the sons of colonists to be Indian missionaries. Good) Tj T*
(Doctor Wheelock had been a friend of grandfather Ledyard?s, and something) Tj T*
(or other had recalled to his mind the fair-haired boy who he had seen) Tj T*
(playing about the old man?s house at Hartford. He would make a missionary) Tj T*
(of the lad, and send him forth to comfort the copper-skinned of the) Tj T*
(elect. A letter arrived offering John the status of a free pupil destined) Tj T*
(to the Indian field. Sulky and ancient nag were presently produced from) Tj T*
(somewhere, perhaps from John?s own pocket, for he had just inherited a) Tj T*
(tiny legacy; the uncle and aunt waved farewell, a whip cracked in the) Tj T*
(air, and John and his sulky vanished over the hills and far away.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(At Dartmouth College, he liked to act in plays, and clad in robes) Tj T*
(of Yankee calico, strutted about as the Numidian Prince, Syphax, in) Tj T*
(Mr. Addison?s ?Tragedy of Cato.? A savour of old-fashioned rhetoric) Tj T*
