(and magniloquence made its way from these plays into John?s mind,) Tj T*
(and coloured his letters and his language all his life. He liked the) Tj T*
(out-of-doors, and on one occasion induced a group of comrades to climb) Tj T*
(with him to the top of a neighboring height, and spend the night on) Tj T*
(evergreen boughs strewn on the floor of deep holes dug in the snow.) Tj T*
(Doctor Wheelock nodded an enthusiastic consent; he saw in John?s) Tj T*
(adventure fine training in hardship for his future missionaries! Letters) Tj T*
(of classmates paint Ledyard as restless, impatient of the dry bones) Tj T*
(of discipline, authoritative on occasion, and more a man with devoted) Tj T*
(cronies than one largely and carelessly popular. All other Dartmouth) Tj T*
(memories have faded in the epic glow of the adventurer?s flight from his) Tj T*
(Alma Mater.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(He came to college in a sulky, he left it an even more adventurous way.) Tj T*
(In the spring of 1773, the sound of the axe rings in the Dartmouth woods.) Tj T*
(Presently comes a shout, a great, crackling crash, and the sound and) Tj T*
(tremor of a heavy blow upon the earth. John Ledyard and his cronies have) Tj T*
(just felled a giant pine standing close by the bank of the Connecticut) Tj T*
(River. From this log, the homespun undergraduates fashion a dug-out) Tj T*
(canoe, fifty feet long and three feet wide, a veritable barge of a canoe,) Tj T*
(and once the digging and hacking is done with, John himself weaves at the) Tj T*
(stern of the craft a kind of shelter-bower of willow wands. Word passes) Tj T*
(among the lads to be at the river early in the morning.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The spring in northern New England is no gracious and gradual awakening,) Tj T*
(it is shy, even timid, of approach, and there are times when the new) Tj T*
(leaves and petals have quite the air of children who have run out of) Tj T*
(the house on a winter?s day. Then comes a sudden night of warmth and) Tj T*
(southwest wind, smells of wet earth and the sound of flooded streams fill) Tj T*
(all the dark, a rushing spirit of fertility shakes the land, and the) Tj T*
(rising sun reveals a world hurrying on to June. A dangerous spring in a) Tj T*
(Puritan land, for flesh and spirit are taken unawares, and swept off to) Tj T*
(the shrines of gods who have never made a covenant with man.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Such a spring it was, as the forest undergraduates gathered at the huge) Tj T*
(dug-out under the slanting light of early day, and watched their friend) Tj T*
(carry supplies to his canoe. John first put aboard a provender of dried) Tj T*
(venison and cornmeal, then a huge bearskin for a coverlet, and last of) Tj T*
(all two strangely assorted books, a Greek New Testament and the poems of) Tj T*
(Ovid. The truant Yankee sophomore steps into his canoe. A long halloo,) Tj T*
(a push all together, and the craft has slid off into the river, which,) Tj T*
(clear of ice and swollen by a thousand mountain streams, is rushing past) Tj T*
(their little college and on into the world. The current seizes the canoe;) Tj T*
(the wet paddle blade flashes in the cool sun; John masters the swirl) Tj T*
(with his strength and woodsman skill, and the future vagabond disappears) Tj T*
(on the way to his fantastic destiny. Little does the truant know that) Tj T*
(in January and February, 1787, a forlorn, penniless but indomitable) Tj T*
(traveller will accomplish one of the most amazing feats ever performed by) Tj T*
(mortal man, a fifteen hundred mile trudge through an unknown country deep) Tj T*
(in arctic snow and cold, and that the vagabond will be John Ledyard.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The mystery of his truancy remains to puzzle the world. For after all,) Tj T*
