(why had he run away? In abandoning Dartmouth, he had locked behind him) Tj T*
(the one door to an education which had opened to him in his obscurity.) Tj T*
(John Ledyard?s contemporaries said simply that the spring was racing) Tj T*
(in his blood, and that the born vagabond had been unable to control a) Tj T*
(vagabond urge. There is a world of truth in the reply, but not quite) Tj T*
(all the truth. The present day, with greater historical perspective,) Tj T*
(will have it that this fair-haired lad was not really a scion of the) Tj T*
(seaboard generations of transplanted Englishmen, but a son of the new,) Tj T*
(native-born, and native-minded culture which was springing up in the) Tj T*
(hearts of Americans during the last half of the eighteenth century. This) Tj T*
(lad is no spiritual kinsman of harsh and merciless Endecott; his place) Tj T*
(is with Daniel Boone and the lords of the frontier. But at Dartmouth,) Tj T*
(the seventeenth century sat in the seat of power, for, intellectually,) Tj T*
(Wheelock was a contemporary of Cotton Mather; the two dominies would have) Tj T*
(talked the same Canaanitish jargon, and shared an identical attitude to) Tj T*
(life. But young John was of different stuff, and, moreover, he was in) Tj T*
(certain ways, curiously modern. His flight from Dartmouth thus becomes) Tj T*
(a bit of vagabondage hiding an instinctive recoil, for had he accepted) Tj T*
(a missionary career, the seventeenth century would have claimed him) Tj T*
(forever for its own.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Down the Connecticut River floats the log canoe, carrying a young New) Tj T*
(Englander from theology under Oliver Cromwell to adventure under George) Tj T*
(the Third.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(II) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Now came difficulties and explanations, and John cut the knot by going to) Tj T*
(sea. Four years later, at the end of a voyage, a young American seaman) Tj T*
(walks the narrow streets of London?s ?Sailortown.? John Ledyard is now) Tj T*
(twenty-five years old, life has done little with him, and he has done) Tj T*
(little with life; his friends at home are beginning to regard him as) Tj T*
(something of a ne?er-do-well, and the pockets of his sailor breeches are) Tj T*
(emptier than ever.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In ?Sailortown? an April sun is shining, the dank smell of the Thames) Tj T*
(mingles with wood smoke from the hearths, and there is a sound of men?s) Tj T*
(voices and a clink of glasses at the doors of mariner?s inns. John steps) Tj T*
(into a tavern, and hears news which fires his imagination, and sets his) Tj T*
(blood to racing. Captain James Cook, the great navigator and explorer,) Tj T*
(is about to make a third voyage to the South Seas, and ships are being) Tj T*
(prepared and loaded for the expedition. With characteristic audacity,) Tj T*
(John hurries directly to the Captain at his lodgings in Chelsea Hospital,) Tj T*
(and boldly requests to be allowed to go. His colonial directness pleases,) Tj T*
(and John Ledyard walks back to London, no longer an obscure American) Tj T*
(seaman, but a corporal of His Majesty?s Marines attached to Cook?s own) Tj T*
(vessel, the _Discovery_.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The two ships of the expedition, the old _Resolution_ and the new) Tj T*
(_Discovery_, sailed from England on July 12th, 1776, bound for the South) Tj T*
(Pacific by the Cape of Good Hope.) Tj T*
