(I) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A little over a hundred years ago the learned world of fashionable London) Tj T*
(was profoundly moved by the arrival of eventful news. After having been) Tj T*
(sealed to Europeans for some four thousand years, one of the great) Tj T*
(pyramids of Egypt had at length been opened, and torch in hand, a modern) Tj T*
(man had walked the untrodden dust of the oven-hot and silent galleries.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Now that all three pyramids stand open to the world, and tourists with) Tj T*
(green sun-goggles and parasols hesitate and giggle at the forbidding) Tj T*
(entrances, it is difficult to believe that the interiors should have been) Tj T*
(so recently a mystery. Save for a few measurements, however, the first) Tj T*
(years of the nineteenth century knew no more about the great Pyramids) Tj T*
(than the Renaissance had known; all was tradition, legend and conjecture.) Tj T*
(Of the familiar giants at Gizeh, only one, the Great Pyramid of Cheops,) Tj T*
(was open, and this but very partially so, for the famous well and the) Tj T*
(lower galleries were clogged with rubbish and débris. The second pyramid,) Tj T*
(that of Chephren, and the third, that of Mycerinus, were apparently solid) Tj T*
(mountains of limestone blocks with no sign whatsoever of an opening or a) Tj T*
(door.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It is scarce possible to exaggerate the hold which these locked giants) Tj T*
(had maintained on the imagination of mankind. The pilgrim of the middle) Tj T*
(ages thought them the granaries of Joseph, and stared at them with) Tj T*
(reverence; the conquering Arab called them the palaces of kings, sleeping) Tj T*
(enchanted in moated halls whose lamps were hollow emeralds.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(All tales, however, agreed upon one point,?that the pyramids concealed) Tj T*
(a treasure. The Arabic conquerors of Egypt had already sought it, and) Tj T*
(one of them, the tenth century caliph, Al Mamun, baffled by the masonry) Tj T*
(of the third pyramid, had actually made a vain and lunatic attempt to) Tj T*
(destroy the entire edifice. So kings passed, and emperors and sultans and) Tj T*
(great ages of historic time, but the sunrise still rolled up the veiling) Tj T*
(mist from the great plain of Egypt, revealing the vast, solemn geometry) Tj T*
(of the masters of the Nile. What treasure, what strange secret lay within) Tj T*
(these stones? Who would be the first to enter them? What would he find?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: BELZONI]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In the year 1778, Jacopo Belzoni, a worthy barber of Padua, and Teresa) Tj T*
(his wife, were rejoicing at the arrival of a son. They had christened) Tj T*
(him Giovanni Battista, or ?Gianbattista? for short. Had a soothsayer of) Tj T*
(ancient Egypt appeared by the cradle, and revealed the infant?s destiny,) Tj T*
(the good _tonsore_ would have surely opened his mouth and dropped his) Tj T*
(shears. For the soothsayer would have said something like this:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(?This child will be a juggler at theatres and village fairs, a scholar,) Tj T*
(an author, and a traveller. For thirty-seven years, life will toss him) Tj T*
(about as a juggler tosses a ball in the air, but then his opportunity) Tj T*
