(will come, he will win fame in a strange land, and solve the most) Tj T*
(romantic of all mysteries.?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The adventurous tale begins, strangely enough, in a monastery. The worthy) Tj T*
(Jacopo had fathered a brood of fourteen,?something had to be found for) Tj T*
(each and every one of them, and in the distribution young Giovanni) Tj T*
(Battista was handed over to the church. He was to find a place in the) Tj T*
(world for himself as a monk. From the parental dwelling on a by-street) Tj T*
(in Padua, the boy, still in his teens, walked the ancient highways of) Tj T*
(Umbria to the house of a monastic order in Rome. Somewhere in the old) Tj T*
(papal city, behind an encircling wall, his days of boyhood and youth) Tj T*
(began before the dawn with the clangour of a monastery bell, and ended) Tj T*
(with the echoing cave of a darkened church, the golden, pin-point flames) Tj T*
(of altar lamps, and the solemn chanting of the offices.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Years pass, years of quiet and withdrawal from the world. Of a sudden) Tj T*
(comes alarming news, the pot of the Revolution has boiled over, the) Tj T*
(French are crossing the frontiers and invading Italy. Presently there are) Tj T*
(disorders in Rome and a descent of French troops upon the city; the bells) Tj T*
(are silenced, the monasteries closed or seized for barracks, and the) Tj T*
(monks harried out into the street.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Among the monks thus compelled to abandon the religious life was) Tj T*
(Gianbattista Belzoni. The Paduan novice had grown up into a giant, a) Tj T*
(colossus even, for he now stood six feet seven inches in height, and) Tj T*
(was broadly and solidly built in the same proportion. And not only did) Tj T*
(Gianbattista have a giant?s strength, he had also the pride and the) Tj T*
(sense of decorum which accompany a giant stature. Those who are born of) Tj T*
(average height little know how huge is the influence of great stature on) Tj T*
(its possessor?s conduct and character! He who is born a Titan must act) Tj T*
(the Titan; a frolicsome colossus is an outrage to Nature. Gianbattista,) Tj T*
(moreover, though of Paduan birth, was of Roman stock, and Romans have to) Tj T*
(this day an eye for dignity. Brown eyed, and black-brown of hair, with a) Tj T*
(giant?s mildness, a giant?s decorum, and an Italian?s grace of address,) Tj T*
(young Gianbattista was a figure for Michelangelo.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Walking with a giant?s disdain through the rabble of soldiers and) Tj T*
(revolutionists jeering by the monastery gate, the young monk passed forth) Tj T*
(into the world.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The homeless young Titan, he was only 22, may well have wondered what) Tj T*
(was now to become of him. At the monastery school he had chanced to make) Tj T*
(a special study of the science of hydraulics, but that was hardly a) Tj T*
(knowledge to be peddled about in those uncertain times. Having no choice,) Tj T*
(therefore, he fell back on his physical strength, and set about earning) Tj T*
(his living as a juggler and a Hercules of village fairs. From Italy the) Tj T*
(showman monk made his way through Germany, and then through Holland) Tj T*
(to the various kingdoms of the British Isles. Finding life pleasant in) Tj T*
(England, he settled down there, and spent the Napoleonic years amusing) Tj T*
(his hosts and becoming something of an Englishman.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(For the next ten years, his life is that of an Italian mountebank in) Tj T*
