(England. The English knew the huge, serious, well-mannered foreigner as) Tj T*
(?Signor? Belzoni; they saw him in their pantomimes and at Bartholomew) Tj T*
(Fair. He had a booth at the fair, and amid the smell of black puddings) Tj T*
(sizzling on the fire, and the shouts and cries of barrow vendors and) Tj T*
(showmen, our Signor delighted the London rabble with feats of strength) Tj T*
(and dexterity. His favorite show was a spectacle called ?Samson,? an) Tj T*
(edifying Biblical affair in whose course Belzoni pulled down the pillars) Tj T*
(of a stage temple with the most blood-curdling roars, crash, dust and) Tj T*
(general uproar. At Sadlers Wells Theatre, to quote an old play bill, his) Tj T*
