(In 1891 an infection of the knee obliged him to return to Europe, an) Tj T*
(operation failed to check the malady, and in November he found that) Tj T*
(timelessness which he once pictured as ?the sea fled with the sun.?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(As a personage, Rimbaud remains the most mysterious of all vagabonds.) Tj T*
(The ceaseless, embittered, eager search for something that was his) Tj T*
(life,?what shall be its last interpretation? Did he seek something which) Tj T*
(had fled him, or something to replace the thing which had fled? From the) Tj T*
(Latin quarter of the 80?s, with its book shops, its old dank houses, and) Tj T*
(drizzling rain of the cloudy Parisian spring to the lifeless oven of) Tj T*
(Aden, his mind had known but one aim, and that an aim unlike any other) Tj T*
(sought by the great vagabonds. No answer may be found by scanning the) Tj T*
(poetry, for Rimbaud the poet and Rimbaud the Somali trader were two men.) Tj T*
(Active, nervous, intellectual, difficult and often utterly unpleasant) Tj T*
(and unsympathetic, he wanders about his bales of goods in the warehouse) Tj T*
(shadows, a mysterious and intriguing figure. After all, though he did) Tj T*
(not find the answer he sought?who does??he found activity, and for him) Tj T*
(activity was the soul?s rest.) Tj T*
